<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rao Jodha</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1416">1416</a>–<a href="/years/1489">1489</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Rao Jodha was the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rathore">Rathore</a> ruler of Marwar who founded the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jodhpur">Jodhpur</a> in 1459. After recovering his family's capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mandore">Mandore</a> from Mewar, he built a new capital on a rocky hill nearby, with the great fort of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mehrangarh">Mehrangarh</a>. His many sons founded other states, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rao-bika">Rao Bika</a>, who established Bikaner.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rao-jodha.jpg" alt="Rao Jodha" width="220" height="345" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown artist · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rao_Jodha.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Jodha was the eldest son of Rao Ranmal (Rao Ridmal), who took the throne of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mandore">Mandore</a> in 1427 and also served as administrator of Mewar for Maharana Mokal and then for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rana-kumbha">Rana Kumbha</a>. When Ranmal was murdered at Chittor by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sisodia">Sisodias</a>, Jodha escaped, pursued by Kumbha's uncle Rawat Chunda, whose army killed almost all his soldiers. The weakened <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rathore">Rathore</a> chiefs rallied to him, and on 27 October 1439 he was proclaimed Rao at Kavani, where Punya Raj, son of the holy woman Karni Mata, applied the Raj-Tilak on her behalf.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>For years Jodha gathered strength while a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sisodia">Sisodia</a> garrison under Narbad held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mandore">Mandore</a>. Tradition says Karni Mata told him when to strike. In 1453 he marched with 700 horsemen and 10,000 foot soldiers; with the help of his confidant Kalu Mangalia, a thousand of his men got into the citadel at night and opened it to the rest, and by sunrise Mandore was his. His brother Rao Kandhal then took Chokri, Merta and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ajmer">Ajmer</a>. A treaty restored peace with Mewar, fixed the boundary, and Jodha's daughter married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rana-kumbha">Rana Kumbha</a>'s son Raimal.</p><p>Allied with the Deora and Bhati clans, Jodha won Merta, Phalodi, Pokran, Bhadrajun, Sojat, Jaitaran, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siwana">Siwana</a> and parts of Nagaur and Godwar from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a>. He took Chapar Drona from the Mohils and Fatehpur from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pashtuns">Pathans</a>, and gave frontier lands to his sons: Duda held Merta, Satal founded Satalmer, Suja secured Sojat and Karamsi founded Khimsar. Jodhpur lay on the trade route from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a>, which carried <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a>, opium, sandalwood and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a>. In 1489, after Sarang Khan, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a>'s governor of Hissar, killed Kandhal, Jodha and Bika defeated the Delhi army at Dronpur.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Rao Jodha died on 6 April 1489, aged 73. A struggle among his sons followed; Rao Satal ruled from 1489 to 1491 and was succeeded by Rao Suja. Mehrangarh, which he began in 1459 on a hill 125 metres high, was enlarged by his successors, among them Maharaja Jaswant Singh and Maharaja Ajit Singh, who raised its Fatehpol gate in 1707. His wife Rani Hadi Jasmade built the Ranisar tank, now within the fort's walls.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Rao Jodha” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1465 Rao Bika, a son of <b>Rao Jodha</b> of Marwar, led followers north into the Jangladesh region of the Thar and carved out a kingdom from local Jat and Bhati lands.</blockquote><a href="/state/bikaner-state">Bikaner State</a></li><li><blockquote>Rao Chunda took Mandore around 1395, and in 1459 <b>Rao Jodha</b> founded Jodhpur and the hilltop fort of Mehrangarh as a new capital.</blockquote><a href="/state/jodhpur-state">Jodhpur State</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mandore">Mandore</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mehrangarh">Mehrangarh Fort</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=26.35,73.03&amp;year=1463" aria-label="Mandore on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6477 1196 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Rao Jodha"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6927 1466h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6927 1466h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=26.35,73.03&amp;year=1463">What was here in 1463? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
